"Journal Metrics | Journal of High School Science (https://jhss.scholasticahq.com/pages/776-metrics)\nciteturn222search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; \"As a high school researcher, I’ve had a really positive experience in beginning to navigate the world of scholarly research through the Journal of High School Science. ... \"We were directed to submit our article for JHSS as it is a reputed high school journal with a thorough peer review process, which makes the content more authentic and presentable. ... As an early-career researcher, I am proud that my first paper found its home at JHSS, a journal that values author development alongside research quality.”\n\nWe welcome submissions from students of all backgrounds, schools, and educational experiences. Our review process is both rigorous and thorough, offering valuable feedback to help improve each paper while also providing mentorship and guidance to the authors. Publications in the Journal of High School Science are based solely on merit, ensuring that every paper is selected for its quality and contribution to the field. Below is a selection of papers published in the journal for 2024-2025,, including work from students at some of the Top 200 high schools in the United States, as recognized by U.S. News & World Report.\n\nFeatured Manuscripts for 2024-2025\nBasis Phoenix High School, Phoenix, AZ, Ranked 45th , Biomarkers in Extracellular Vesicles\nThomas S. Wootton High School, Rockville, MD, Ranked 196th, Pediatric Clinical trials\nDougherty Valley High School, San Ramon, CA, Ranked 155th, QA generation using AI\nLynbrook High School, San Jose, CA, Ranked 82nd, CAR-T cells for Glioblastoma multiforme\nThomas Jefferson High School, Alexandria, VA, Ranked 14th, ML for CVD\nTesla STEM High School, Redmond, WA, Ranked 3rd, Biomarkers for ALS\nUnion County Magnet High School, Scotch Plains, NJ, Ranked 95th, Monitoring Coronavirus\nStuyvesant High School, New York, NY, Ranked 26th, Electrolytic production of CuCl_{2}\nAdlai E Stevenson High School, Lincolnshire, IL, Ranked 194th, Risk factors for Youth suicide\nConestoga High School, Berwyn, PA, Ranked 141st, Songs affect the sweetness of Chocolate\nBergen County Technical Schools, Teterboro, NJ, Ranked 64th, Exon skipping\nInterlake High School, Bellevue, WA, Ranked 181st, Factors for financial success\nTesla STEM High School, Redmond, WA, Ranked 3rd, Motor Imagery Classifiers\n\nPeer Reviews ▼\n\nPeer reviews for published manuscripts are PUBLIC\n\nWe believe we are the First, and as yet, the only, High School Journal that makes the reviewers’ comments for published manuscripts, publicly available. These Peer Reviews available from Issue 3, 2024 onwards. We will work backwards as time and resources permit. This is in line with emerging best-practices where Independent non-profit scientific publishers such as elife, have adopted this paradigm. The objective is to provide future authors and submitters with information that they can use to improve their manuscripts pre-submission per the best practices of scientific discourse, analysis, logic, presentation and erudition. Submitters can browse all the published manuscripts (from issue 3, 2024, as of now), find one that is most similar to their work and peruse the Peer Reviews for that manuscript online. The Peer review trail will typically be a supplementary file designated as “data sets/Files”.\n\nReadability Scores ▼\n\nReadability Scores: Manuscripts published in the Journal display a high level of written English articulation\n\nAccepted and published manuscripts showcase writing ability that must; of necessity; possess a minimum level of sophistication so as to present ideas and content that are/is scientifically valid, meaningful and makes a contribution to the existing corpus of knowledge in the field. It is hence often necessary to pivot to clearer technical explanation at the expense of ease of writing. The readability scores of the manuscripts published from 2024 – to date, display this proclivity. The Flesch Kincaid, Gunning Fog, SMOG and ARI scores are 29.2, 19.8, 17.0 and 22.6 respectively; reflective of content understandability at a college level. Accepted manuscripts – through a combination of the authors’ ability and value-added copyediting – thus display appropriate English comprehension, vocabulary and sentence structure necessary for excellent scientific and technical erudition.\n\nStudent Demographics ▼\n\nPublished authors originate from High Schools around the world\n\nUSA, Canada, Mexico, Argentina, Peru, Austria, Switzerland, Italy, Greece, Romania, Turkey, Georgia, Khazakstan, India, Republic of Korea, Taiwan, Singapore, Thailand, Hong Kong, China, and Australia.\n\nBest Manuscripts ▼\n\nBest Review and Original papers for 10(2), 2026 as judged by the Editorial board with input from reviewpaperai. Certificates awarded to\n\nPatel, Anaya, 10 (2): 502-520\nAshraf, Dylan, 10 (2): 521-545\n\nFor earlier awarded papers, look for the ‘Best Manuscript Stamp’ on the paper online.\n\nTestimonials ▼\n\n\"The Journal of High School Science was extremely thorough in reviewing my review paper with a quick turnaround time. They provided clear feedback and valuable suggestions on areas for improvement before publication. This experience has allowed me to experience the process of submitting a manuscript to a journal, which will be indefinitely useful for future research endeavors.\"\n\nCollingwood School, 70 Morven Drive, West Vancouver, British Columbia, V7S 3H4, Canada.\n\n__________________________________________________________________________________________\n\n\"I had a great experience with the Journal of High School Science. JHSS has a very professional and transparent peer-review process. The editors and reviewers all treated my work seriously, and their responses were prompt. I would highly recommend JHSS to high schoolers who want to publish their works.\"\n\n\n\"The Journal of High School Science sets an exceptional standard as an academic publishing journal. The peer review process was impressively thorough: reviewers offered detailed comments on every error and ambiguity in my drafts. Their constructive feedback gave me the opportunity to refine my work step-by-step, and allowed me to gain a deeper understanding of academic writing. The review process was smooth and highly effective, thanks to the editors’ timely responses to resubmissions and their consistent encouragement throughout the revision process. This supportive and professional atmosphere not only helped me grow as an academic writer but also encouraged me to continue pursuing research to create a meaningful and positive impact on the world.\"\n\nPiedmont Hills High School, 1377 Piedmont Rd, San Jose, CA 95132, USA.\n\n__________________________________________________________________________________________\n\n\"Submitting my manuscript to the Journal of High School Science was the best decision. The submission guidelines were straightforward and easy to follow, and I really appreciated that other than the Abstract and References, I had a lot of leeway to shape the manuscript in a way that reflected my own voice. It made the process feel extremely empowering.\n\nThe peer review process was incredibly rigorous. While the initial feedback I received was quite critical, questioning even the entire premise of my paper, I now recognize how essential that was in helping me present my research more clearly and convincingly. The reviewers challenged me to question my own thinking, refine my arguments, and ultimately grow as a scholar. I am grateful to have been treated like a serious contributor to the academic conversation.\n\nThe journal’s response was always prompt and efficient. In fact, I must admit that I was usually the one delaying the process with late revisions — apologies for that!\n\n\n\"The Journal of High School Science uses a very thorough, clear, and direct peer review process: I was given specific constructive criticism and it was easy to understand what could be improved about my work. Additionally, the editor responded very quickly and was easy to work with. The editing process certainly led to me having a better understanding of what makes this type of paper most informative and helpful to the reader.\"\n\nLower Merion High School, 315 E Montgomery Ave, Ardmore, PA 19003, USA.\n\n__________________________________________________________________________________________\n\n\"As a first-time author, my experience with JHSS was exceptional. The initial peer review was detailed and helped improve my paper’s clarity and structure. After revisions, the second round was completed quickly, and the team became extremely responsive to emails. What truly set JHSS apart was how the editors helped re-guide my paper and elevate its novelty, something many other publishers wouldn’t do. I’m incredibly grateful for the support and insight I gained throughout the process.\"\n\nDublin High School, 8151 Village Pkwy, Dublin, CA 94568, USA.\n\n__________________________________________________________________________________________\n\n\"When I first finished my paper and submitted it, I was incredibly nervous about the peer-review process. I had read online that many journals take months to respond and even longer to officially publish. However, after submitting to JHSS, I realized their peer-review process was not only thorough (genuinely improving the quality of my work) but also quick with responses. This allowed me to refine my research, forcing me to review my data more critically while also teaching me about the journal submission process. I highly recommend JHSS to any high schoolers looking to receive careful peer feedback and publish a high-quality paper.\"\n\nUpper Canada College, 200 Lonsdale Rd, Toronto, ON M5P 1K7, Canada.\n\n__________________________________________________________________________________________\n\n\"Publishing in the Journal of High School Science was a smooth process! They make it very easy to know what is happening with your submission, such as whether they have approved your manuscript or whether they want you to answer some questions first. The questions help make sure that your manuscript is the best it can be before final edits. I benefited from this experience as it was my first time publishing my scientific research and it was a very efficient process.\"\n\nEastlake High School, 305 211th PL SE, Sammamish, WA 98074, USA.\n\n__________________________________________________________________________________________\n\n\"JHSS provided a thorough and constructive peer review of my manuscript, offering valuable feedback that significantly improved the quality of my work. The time from initial submission to first decision was about a month, faster than I had expected, and the turnaround for subsequent revision rounds was quicker. Overall, the experience helped me gain a deeper understanding of my research area through the reviewers’ insights and also taught me a lot about the academic publication process.\"\n\nWestlake High School, 100 N Lakeview Canyon Rd. Westlake Village, CA 91362, USA.\n\n__________________________________________________________________________________________\n\n\"Submitting to the Journal of High School Science was a very smooth and efficient process. The editors provided specific, insightful, and meaningful feedback, and even recommended additional sources for further reading, which helped add more depth and professionalism to our paper. The review and publication process were also impressively fast. We would highly recommend this journal to other students looking for an efficient and educational publication process. Thank you again to the editors and reviewers of the Journal of High School Science!\"\n\nFairview High School, 1515 Greenbriar Blvd, Boulder, CO 80305, USA.\nPeak to Peak Charter School, 800 Merlin Dr, Lafayette, CO 80026, USA.\n\n__________________________________________________________________________________________\n\n\"I had a great experience with the Journal of High School Science. This was my first manuscript, and I’m very glad I chose this journal for submission. I received a decision about six weeks after submitting, which was much sooner than I expected. This gave me ample time to address the comments. Each subsequent review was very timely. It took less than 48 hours for the responses to my explanations to come in. Additionally, the review process was very thorough. I wasn’t just asked questions or given suggestions to strengthen the manuscript, but the reviewer also provided linked resources to help me address their points. By the end of the process, my manuscript was more detailed and focused, and I was able to clearly acknowledge and address its limitations. I will be able to use the skills I gained here for any future publications.\"\n\nBasis Phoenix High School, 13231 N 22nd St Phoenix, AZ 85022, USA.\n\n__________________________________________________________________________________________\n\n\"Choosing the Journal of High School Science for publication was a great decision. JHSS offered me a great publishing experience with very insightful comments and revisions. My work was taken serious, the responses were prompt, and overall, it felt like a group effort rather than a job. I’m deeply grateful to JHSS for their thorough guidance and genuine investment in my growth, and I would highly recommend publishing with them.\"\n\nThe Hotchkiss School, 11 Interlaken Rd, Lakeville, CT 06039, USA.\n\n__________________________________________________________________________________________\n\n\"I was very impressed by the thoroughness of the peer review process at JHSS. The reviewers provided detailed, constructive comments that significantly improved the clarity and accessibility of our manuscript for the broader audience. The response time was excellent, and the entire process was efficient and well-communicated.\nTaipei American School, No. 800, Lane 754, Section 6, Zhongshan N Rd, Shilin District, Taipei City, Taiwan 11152.\n\n________________________________________________________________________________________\n\n\"We were directed to submit our article for JHSS as it is a reputed high school journal with a thorough peer review process, which makes the content more authentic and presentable. I am highly impressed by the professionalism of the editorial board and their promptness in attending to the authors' queries in spite of their busy schedules. The peer review of our article was a revelation, as it was very thorough and insightful, like any other leading scientific medical journal. The whole exercise from submission to the final publication was an excellent experience, making our project much more refined. We would not hesitate to recommend this journal for the publication of their work to every high school student across the world.\"\n\nThe Future Kids Global School, Base Complex, 88-1-26, Kavalagoyya Road, near ONGC, Pidimgoyya, Rajamahendravaram, Andhra Pradesh 533103, India.\nWestlake High School, 825 Westlake Dr, Thornwood, NY 10594, USA.\n\n_________________________________________________________________________________________\n\n\"Publishing with the Journal of High School Science has been an extraordinarily positive and intellectually enriching experience. I was deeply impressed by the high level of professionalism and scholarly depth of the peer reviewers. In particular, their perceptive feedback regarding the application and theoretical nuance of Markovian versus non-Markovian dynamics prompted me to probe much deeper into my analysis. This rigorous critique helped me address critical theoretical subtleties, substantially sharpen my argumentation, and make the overall framework far more comprehensive and persuasive. Combined with the editorial team’s efficiency, clear guidance, and supportive communication throughout revisions, the entire process truly elevated the quality of my manuscript. I am sincerely grateful for the journal's commitment to fostering rigorous scholarship among young researchers.\"\n\nShanghai Starriver Bilingual School, 2588 Jindu Rd., Shanghai, P. R. China 201108\n\nCurrent Review Timeline ▼\n\nManuscripts currently being reviewed\n\nNow (10-18-2026) reviewing manuscripts submitted on 9-17-2026 . This information is updated every weekend. Average days for a review of your submitted manuscript (regardless of whether a first time submission or a revised submission) (changes every weekend), 31 days\nPlease note that we are experiencing a substantial increase in submissions. Consequently, review timelines are expected to lengthen significantly over the summer and into the fall.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWeakly-Supervised Deepfake Localization in Diffusion-Generated Images (https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/WACV2024/papers/Tantaru_Weakly-Supervised_Deepfake_Localization_in_Diffusion-Generated_Images_WACV_2024_paper.pdf)\nciteturn222search12 [wordlim: 200] eoneata@bitdefender.com ... University Politehnica of Bucharest  \ndan.oneata@gmail.com\nWeakly-supervised deepfake localization in diffusion-generated images\n\nDragoș-Constantin Tântaru  \nBitdefender  \ndtantaru@bitdefender.com\n\nElisabeta Oneață  \nBitdefender  \neoneata@bitdefender.com\n\nDan Oneață  \nUniversity Politehnica of Bucharest  \ndan.oneata@gmail.com\n\nAbstract\n\nThe remarkable generative capabilities of denoising diffusion models have raised new concerns regarding the authenticity of the images we see every day on the Internet. However, the vast majority of existing deepfake detection models are tested against previous generative approaches (e.g. GAN) and usually provide only a “fake” or “real” label per image. We believe a more informative output would be to augment the per-image label with a localization map indicating which regions of the input have been manipulated. To this end, we frame this task as a weakly-supervised localization problem and identify three main categories of methods (based on either explanations, local scores or attention), which we compare on an equal footing by using the Xception network as the common backbone architecture. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTeam | Bitdefender AI Research (https://bit-ml.github.io/team/)\nciteturn222search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; email (eoneata@bitdefender.com) · website\n\nImage: Dan Oneață\n\nImage: Elisabeta Oneață\n\n### Elisabeta Oneață\n\nI have obtained my PhD in computer vision from the School of Advanced Studies of the Romanian Academy (SCOSAAR), with the thesis “Perceptual Models for Computational Visual Analysis”, under the supervision of Prof. Dr. Cristian Sminchisescu. My work focused on 3D human pose estimation and perception in monocular images and videos. Currently I am interested in automatic detection of deepfake visual content.\n\nemail (eoneata@bitdefender.com) · website\n\nImage: Dan Oneață\n\n### Dan Oneață\n\nI received my Ph.D. from Université Grenoble Alpes, where I worked on automatic action recognition in videos. During my postdoctoral research, I started investigating how to integrate vision with other modalities, such as speech and language. This work showed how visual grounding can help with tasks like speech recognition or language documentation, and how multimodal models relate to human learning. At Bitdefender, I apply multimodal learning to detect deepfakes across various media, including images, audio, and video.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDan Oneață (https://doneata.bitbucket.io/index.html)\nciteturn222search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Image: Picture of Dan Oneata ... My name is Dan and I'm a research scientist at the University politehnica of Bucharest in the SpeeD lab and Bitdefender. ... Bucharest CV reading group · Bucharest, Romania · February, 2018\n## Dan Oneață\n\n* * *\n\nImage: Picture of Dan Oneata\n\noneata\n\nHello and welcome to my web-page! My name is Dan and I'm a research scientist at the University politehnica of Bucharest in the SpeeD lab and Bitdefender. Previously, I have worked at Fordaq and Eloquentix. I have received my PhD in computer vision and machine learning from the Université Grenoble Alpes, where I was fortunate to work under the supervision of Cordelia Schmid and Jakob Verbeek. Before that I have received an MSc in artificial intelligence from the University of Edinburgh and I did my dissertation under Iain Murray. My research interests include general machine learning techniques and its applications to computer vision, speech, and natural language processing. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCommunity Engagement | Bitdefender AI Research (https://bit-ml.github.io/community/)\nciteturn222search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;   * CVPR'26: Dan Oneață (outstanding reviewer) ... Here at Bitdefender we design and teach deep learning courses with top universities in Bucharest and in-house programs.\n\nWe contribute to the research community through peer review and conference organization. Recent involvement includes:\n\n  * ICML'26: Andrei Manolache (gold reviewer), Antonio Bărbălau (gold reviewer), Elena Burceanu (gold reviewer)\n  * CVPR'26: Dan Oneață (outstanding reviewer)\n  * CoLLAs'26: Elena Burceanu (local chair, Bucharest)\n  * NeurIPS'25: Elena Burceanu (Associate Program Chair)\n\n## Technology transfer projects (PTE)\n\nWe run two Proiecte de Transfer la Operator Economic (PTE):\n\n  * Veridiq: multimodal deepfake detection.\n  * VeriDark: authorship verification and identification benchmarks.\n\n## Meetups\n\nWe run regular research meetups. See the meetups page for the schedule (Google Calendar).\n\n## Deepfake Detection Reading Group\n\nWe host a biweekly Deepfake Detection Reading Group to discuss the latest work in the field of deepfake detection. Sessions are informal (Fridays, online). See the reading group page for the schedule, past sessions, and how to join the mailing list.\n\n## Courses\n\nHere at Bitdefender we design and teach deep learning courses with top universities in Bucharest and in-house programs. We bring students and engineers closer to research with up-to-date, hands-on content.\n\n#### DeepFMI: Learning Deep Neural Networks\n\n2025-2026 · February - April\n\nContact: fbrad@bitdefender.com\n\nMore →\n\n#### Deep Reinforcement Learning @ ACS\n\n2025-2026 · October - December\n\nContact: fgogianu@bitdefender.com\n\nMore →--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEUSIPCO 2026 || Bruges, Belgium || 31 August - 4 September 2026 (https://cmsworkshops.com/eusipco2026/view_paper.php?PaperNum=2086&SessionID=1013)\nciteturn222search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; BForSec-L1.1: FEDGE: A THREE-TIER FEDERATED EDGE FRAMEWORK FOR ATTACK DETECTION IN IOT-ENABLED 5GB NETWORKS Zahraa Ghabriess, ENSTA Institut Polytechnique de Paris, Lebanon; Hassan Harb, American University of the Middle East, Kuwait; Ali Mansour, ENSTA Institut Polytechnique de Paris, France; Koffi Clément Yao, University of Western Brittany, France; Christophe Osswald, ENSTA Institut Polytechnique de Paris, France BForSec-L1.2: ANCHORING THE UNKNOWN: OPEN-SET MODEL ATTRIBUTION VIA PROXY-ANCHOR LEARNING Cristian-Teodor Neamtu, Serban Mihalache, Speech and Dialogue Research Laboratory (SpeeD), POLITEHNICA Bucharest, Romania; Stefan Smeu, Bitdefender, Romania; Dan Oneata, Speech and Dialogue Research Laboratory (SpeeD), POLITEHNICA Bucharest / Bitdefender, Romania; Horia Cucu, Dragos Burileanu, Speech and Dialogue Research Laboratory (SpeeD), POLITEHNICA Bucharest, Romania BForSec-L1.3: AWARE: AUDIO WATERMARKING VIA ADVERSARIAL RESISTANCE TO EDITS Kosta Pavlović, Lazar Stanarević,